<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Nutrition – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Nutrition</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Why Is Nutrition Important?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain how to follow and prepare recipes. Interactive: Respond to peers in group activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain steps to prepare a simple healthy meal (e.g., "To make a Panamanian-style fruit salad, chop fresh pineapples, mangos, and papayas. Then, add a squeeze of orange for extra flavor.")
Can express opinions about food choices (e.g., "I think fruits are the best choice because they have vitamins.")
Can respond to peers' suggestions in group discussions on nutrition (e.g., "If you eat enough fruits and vegetables, you will stay healthy." "Yes, I agree, eating vegetables helps us stay healthy.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students get into pairs. The teacher shows flashcards of various foods (some healthy like "fruits," "vegetables," "grains" and some less healthy like "processed" snacks). Students discuss: "Which of these foods do you eat regularly? Which ones do you prefer and why?" The teacher encourages initial use of vocabulary like "eat," "prefer," "healthy," "balanced diet."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models giving simple advice using "should" and expressing opinions. "I think you should eat more fresh fruits because they have many vitamins." "If you want to have more energy, you should consume whole grains." The teacher focuses on stress in words like "vitamin," "organic," "metabolism" and models the minimal pair /ɪ/ in "bit" and /iː/ in "beat" with words like "digestion" vs. "diet."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'You should eat more vegetables,' does that mean you *must* eat them?" (Expected answer: No, it's advice, not an obligation.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's the difference between a 'healthy' meal and a 'balanced' meal?" (Expected answer: Healthy means good for you, balanced means it includes different nutrients like proteins, carbs, and vegetables.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a dialogue between two friends discussing their favorite healthy recipe and giving each other advice on improving their daily nutrition. The genre is an informal conversation, register is casual, and format is audio input with a transcript.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students understand and extract specific information about healthy food preparation and dietary advice from spoken interaction.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to the dialogue and identify True or False statements about the friends' eating habits and the advice they give. (e.g., "Friend A says you should avoid processed foods. True/False").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen again and match the healthy food suggestions (e.g., "fruits," "vegetables," "hydration") to the benefits mentioned (e.g., "vitamins," "fiber," "digestion").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students identify phrases used for giving advice (e.g., "You should...", "If you...", "I recommend...") and justifications (e.g., "...because it...").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice using modal "should" for advice, first conditional for facts, and adverbial clauses for justification, along with vocabulary related to nutrition (e.g., "nutrition," "benefits," "balanced diet," "vitamins," "minerals," "protein," "carbohydrates," "eat," "improve," "recommend").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Choral Repetition):</b> The teacher displays sentences with blanks related to nutrition. Students individually complete the sentences using "should," the first conditional, or adverbial clauses for justification. (e.g., "You ___ (eat) a balanced breakfast if you ___ (want) to have energy all morning because ___ (it contains) carbohydrates."). After completing, the teacher leads a choral repetition, then individual student repetition to practice pronunciation, especially stress patterns in words like "carbohydrate," "antioxidant."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Substitution Drill with Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a sentence frame like "I think you should [verb: consume/eat] more [noun: fruits/vegetables] because [adverbial clause: they improve digestion/give vitamins]." Students take turns substituting the bracketed words with other vocabulary from the list (e.g., "grains," "protein," "fiber," "healthy," "natural") to create new, grammatically correct and meaningful advice. Peers listen and correct any pronunciation or grammar errors.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Nutrition Q&A):</b> Students work in new pairs. They take turns asking and answering questions about nutrition, using the target language. One student asks, "What should I eat to improve my health?" The other responds, "You should eat more fruits and vegetables. If you eat a mix of foods, it will give you energy because it provides essential nutrients." They justify their advice.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Nutrition")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play in small groups of three (Student A: the client, Student B: the nutrition consultant, Student C: observer/note-taker). Students will present their advice and explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students will role-play a "Healthy Meal Planning Session." Student A describes their current eating habits (e.g., "I usually eat processed snacks and I feel tired"). Student B, the "nutrition consultant," must give specific advice on how to eat healthier, focusing on explaining how to prepare a simple, healthy meal and providing justifications. Student B should use "should," first conditional, and adverbial clauses, incorporating vocabulary like "balanced diet," "nutrients," "fiber," "protein," "hydration," and verbs like "chop," "add," "balance." Student A must respond to the suggestions, asking clarifying questions. Student C observes and provides feedback on language use. Example interaction: Student A: "I want to try a new healthy breakfast. What should I prepare?" Student B: "You should make a fruit smoothie. To prepare it, you chop fresh fruits like mangos and papayas, then blend them with water for good hydration. If you drink a smoothie with organic fruits, you will get more vitamins because it's a superfood." Student A: "Yes, I agree, that sounds good. What if I don't like papaya?" Student B: "Then you should use pineapple instead, it also has great benefits for digestion."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Sociolinguistic Focus:</b> Students practice providing advice on improving nutrition during role-play conversations, discussing the importance of healthy eating habits and how to explain a simple healthy meal. They can briefly touch on differences in healthy eating between family celebrations in Panama (e.g., arroz con pollo) and other healthy staple meals they know.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking performance and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher observes the group role-play during the Performance stage. Student C (the observer) uses a simple peer feedback form to evaluate Student A and Student B's speaking, focusing on their use of target grammar, vocabulary, and interaction skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Speaking):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of multisyllabic words and minimal pairs (e.g., "metabolism," "vitamin," "bit/beat").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of modal "should" for advice.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of first conditional for facts.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of adverbial clauses for justification ("because it...").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words (e.g., "benefits," "balanced diet," "nutrients," "fiber," "hydration").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in explaining steps to prepare a simple healthy meal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to respond to peers' suggestions and maintain a conversation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one piece of healthy eating advice you gave or received today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why is it important to justify your advice when talking about nutrition?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging for you when explaining recipes or giving advice about nutrition today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can speaking about nutrition help you make better food choices in your daily life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students actively participated in the role-play, demonstrating good use of 'should' and basic conditional sentences. Some struggled with consistently using adverbial clauses for justification, often providing simple reasons instead of full clauses. Pronunciation of multi-syllabic words like "carbohydrate" and "metabolism" still needs reinforcement. For the next lesson, I will provide sentence starters for justifications to scaffold this. We also need to explicitly connect more Panamanian cultural foods to healthy alternatives or preparation methods.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This speaking lesson provided students with practice articulating ideas and advice about nutrition. The next lesson, Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), will build on this by having students write their own short paragraphs or a simple healthy recipe with justifications, solidifying the vocabulary and grammatical structures used today into written form.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 minutes</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>